[Music]  so for those of you who are here to hear  about the great economy what I want to  do is to give you a sense firstly um how  their ideas have changed the world now  of course I don't want to say that these  are only economic ideas that have  changed the world but rather it is what  economics has played a part in reshaping  the society in which we live which is  essentially how I approach the book  which is if you look back and look at  the extraordinary number of changes that  have affected our society since the  Industrial Revolution 250 years ago how  we got here and the lessons that history  holds the ideas that have changed our  lives those are worth looking at as we  face our current challenges and think  about really Mark Twain's quote which is  history doesn't repeat itself but it  often rhymes and so I'm going to start  off with some of those ideas have  changed the world and then through the  course of the next half hour go through  the people behind those ideas so the  great thinkers of our time the of the  earlier vintage and how their ideas  maybe could just maybe help us with some  of our challenges today so I'm going to  focus on two really two main ideas the  first one is the idea that has changed  the world was the end of protectionism  and this is in 1846 when the corn laws  were repealed now you remember in 1846  now nobody remembers 1846 but up until  that point mercantilism was the belief  of the day that was the idea that  governs the day which is countries  should run trade surpluses now even  better if you could go and find some  gold and silver  hope with much great surplus then that's  really mercantilism but what changed was  globalization so Britain in 1846  repealed the protectionist legislation  the corn loss which was protecting the  profits of landowners around agriculture  output and instead made globe made  Britain a global internationally focused  economy so globalisation made Britain  the world's biggest trader globalization  enhanced the world's first industrial  nation and gave it a global standing but  that was an idea that had been argued by  classical economists and it wasn't until  Sir Robert Peel the prime minister  changed his mind and agreed with the  economists that the Corn Laws were  repealed and an era of prosperity in the  late Victorian period began and by the  way shortly after the repeal the Corn  Laws Sir Robert Peel lost his  Premiership and then his party was out  in the wilderness for a generation but  the Corn Laws got repealed and the  second big idea that changed the world  is the welfare state so what happened  with the panic of 1873 was a US  financial crisis that spread across the  Atlantic and that was when unemployment  first appeared in the dictionary because  it triggered the first Great Depression  of the nineteenth century known as the  long depression that also was when trade  unionism rose and that was when Marx  made his ideas known and by the early  part of the 20th century 60% of the  world lived in either communist or  socialist regimes so it became a battle  between capitalism and communism it  became a battle between democracy and  the communist regimes which existed then  so think about the Bolshevik Revolution  in 1917 the rise of the Soviet Union  and that caused the economists of the  day to rethink capitalism so capitalism  wasn't working for everyone and this is  when you started to have the late  Victorian say well actually a capitalist  system needs to be revised to suit what  the 20th century needed so the late  Victorians believed in helping the  deserving poor and the arguments who  really made then about how you can  introduce a welfare element into  capitalism and that began to win hearts  and minds and this was where really when  the welfare state the NHS in this  country in continental Europe in the  United States changed the capitalism of  Adam Smith and made it welfare state  capitalism and after World War two it  looked like the tide was beginning to  turn but it wasn't until the fall of the  Berlin Wall and the disintegration of  the Soviet Union in the late 1980s that  really this battle between democracy and  communism between capitalism  well Thursday capitalism this new form  took precedence over the appeal of the  Communists and the socialist regimes of  course we know fast forward to the  present day we've had another American  financial crisis that has spread across  the world and we are now also at a time  when capitalism needs rethinking there's  again a backlash against globalization  the consensus that was forged after the  end of the Cold War seems no longer to  be there so we are now in the 21st  century at the same point about  rethinking the economic system that  needs to be revised to work for all of  us in the 21st century and that's really  the motivation for writing this book for  me which is to learn these lessons of  history and that you tackle the big  issues of today and think about the ways  in which we can change our current  market based economy to work better more  equitably and more focused on the  of growth so looking back to history the  great economist whose ideas I'm gonna  draw on are the ones that did think  about the big questions of the day the  great economists have an earlier vintage  many of them were not economists at all  there were philosophers and so I'll go  through them briefly because I think to  understand ideas we really must  understand the people the person behind  those ideas so these are the ones that I  cover and this is a ruff-ruff mapping as  to where they sit so the Adam Smith  David Ricardo they're sort of more  classical and neoclassical economists  sit here so they believe in the market  I'm driving the economy but they believe  the state has a role in ensuring that  the market is competitive so that's  different then more free market  economists like Friedrich Hayek who is  the motivation behind Thatcherism as  well as the Reagan Revolution in the  1980s and then and by contrast on the  other side is Karl Marx who believed  that the capitalist system will  inevitably lead to a demand for more  egalitarian system and that's the  communist system where the state decides  everything but in between you also have  John Maynard Keynes he sits between the  sort of state and market because Maynard  Keynes convinced us all in the 1930s the  Keynesian revolution that the state  could intervene in the economy in fact  the state had to intervene in the  economy and before waiting for the  long-run as the classical economists  believed when everything would be sorted  out he'll be back in equilibrium and  Kings famously said we're all dead in  the long run and then his disciple who  I'm also going to talk about is Joan  Robinson now Joan Robinson was a  Keynesian disciple in the 1930s where  she made her name the reason I'm going  to talk about her is because her theory  of monopsony helps us to explain our  current challenge with low  ages yr wages so low so she writes about  it in the 1930s she started off the  1930s as the wife of a Cambridge Don by  the end of the 1930s she was the most  prominent female economist a great  economist by the way she was one of the  five Cambridge economist within Keynes's  inner circle and trusted to review the  general theory  she was also married to one of the other  ones and having an affair with another  one so I like to think she had a casting  vote on the general theory but I've  moved her slightly towards the state end  because later her career she disavowed  Keynesianism and she began to admire  communist regimes so in her lectures at  Cambridge she would turn up in  Vietnamese peasant outfit so I sort of  moved her more towards the Marx end and  then finally Douglas North a great  economist that I write about so Douglas  north is the person whose ideas  introduce the importance of institutions  into economics for more debates talks  and interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you

